Reflection
    1. What changed from previous lab?
        - In the previous lab, we only worked with basic REST endpoints that returned simple responses. 
        In this lab, we added database integration, so the application is now able to store and manage real 
        data instead of just returning static values. We also started using Spring Data JPA, which made working 
        with databases much easier compared to writing raw SQL.

    2. Why is a database better?
        - A database is better because it stores data permanently, even if the application stops or restarts. 
        Unlike in-memory data, it does not get deleted when the program closes. It is also more reliable and useful 
        for real applications because it can handle larger amounts of data and organize it properly.

    3. How did JPA help?
        - JPA made database work easier by letting us use Java objects instead of writing SQL queries. 
        Through repositories and entities, we can perform CRUD operations with less code. It also automatically handles 
        the mapping between Java classes and database tables, which saves time and reduces errors.

    4. What changed when switching DB?
        - Based on my experience, when switching from H2 to a cloud-hosted PostgreSQL database, the setup became more advanced 
        because I had to configure an external database connection. Unlike H2, which runs locally inside the project, 
        PostgreSQL required a proper URL, username, password, and SSL configuration.